INDEX -- what is on this disk, and where it came from======================================================Every command here was tested with THIS DISK AS THE ONLY DISK (os9exec/dd/CMDS/sh), no Microware module present anywhere -- no shell, csl, cio ormath.  Note this disk does not "boot": os9exec is the kernel and simply runsa program with this disk mounted as /dd.  *  A STAR before a name means that program needs Microware's trap-handler     modules (cio, and for some csl and math).  It will NOT run on this disk     by itself: it stops with "**** Can't install trap handler ****".  Supply     your own cio and it works -- see DOC/README-CIO for how to set that up.     135 programs are starred: 116 in CMDS, 9 in CMDS/GAMES, 10 in     CMDS/REBUILT and 1 in CMDS/BROKEN.  Of the 494 in CMDS and     CMDS/GAMES, 125 are starred and 369 need nothing but this disk.     Everything unstarred ran standalone.  THREE PROGRAMS ARE BASIC09, not 68000 code: bio, blackjack and  wysetime.  They are I-code modules (type 2, language 2) and need  Microware's runb to interpret them, the way a starred program needs  cio.  Run them by BARE NAME with runb loaded -- `load runb` then  `bio`.  Giving runb a pathname instead raises BASIC09 error 43,  "Unknown procedure", which reads like a broken program and is not.  BASIC09's own codes are 10-80 and are not OS-9 error numbers, which  is why SYS/errmsg has no text for them.These are not broken and they are not going anywhere.  They were compiled theordinary way for the era (cc -qixm), which is a licensing question, not aquality one.Provenance: everything came from the Microware OS-9 hobbyist archive, theHamburg OS-9 Users Group, and other public preservation sites. The originalarchives are kept in CMDS/archives/ -- ed, elvis and grep include full Csource in there. Per-package documentation is in DOC/ (see DOC/README).SOURCES.txt carries fuller per-command provenance and licences.Five commands here are OUR OWN BUILDS, not archive binaries: cat, wc, basenameand dirname were rebuilt from source with the gcc2 toolchain against os9lib,because every archived build of them needs cio; and ls is our repaired buildof GNU fileutils 3.13. Their C source is on the h0 workshop disk.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- WORKS ON THIS DISK (80 commands)------------------------------------------------------  ar             archive librarian (Carl Kreider) -- .ar files  as0            6800/6802 cross-assembler (xasm).  NOT a 68000 assembler --                 see the note below this list  as1            6801/6803 cross-assembler (xasm)  as11           68HC11 cross-assembler (xasm)  as4            6804 cross-assembler (xasm)  as5            6805/68HC05 cross-assembler (xasm)  ascii          ASCII character table  THE as* FAMILY ARE 8-BIT CROSS-ASSEMBLERS, not 68000 ones.  This list  used to call them 68000, 68010, 68040 and 68050 assemblers; they are  6800, 6801, 6804 and 6805.  Their own manual, DOC/xasm/asm.doc, opens  "The IBM PC 6800/01/04/05/09/11 cross assemblers" and names them as*  where * is 0, 1, h1, 4, 5, 9 or 11.  Motorola never made a 68050.  as09 (6809, starred) is the one entry that was always right.  Source  for the whole family is in SRC/xasm -- do0.c, do1.c, do4.c, do5.c,  do9.c, do11.c and their opcode tables.  For 68000 work use as0's  neighbours in this directory: r68 is Microware's, and the disk's own  68k assembly route is described in DOC/README-DOCS.  aterm          ATerm 2.6 -- terminal emulator  atob           ASCII-to-binary decode  basename       strip directory from a pathname -- REBUILT HERE with gcc2  bash           GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12 -- this disk's shell; reads .bashrc  beav           BEAV 1.40 -- Binary Editor And Viewer (needs TERM)  bison          GNU bison 1.19 parser generator -- ADDED; skeletons in /dd/LIB  btoa           Binary-to-ASCII encode  cat            concatenate files -